NAME
       auto.master - the master map of autofs, and the format of its maps

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/auto.master
       /etc/auto.master.d/*.autofs
       map files such as /etc/auto.misc

DESCRIPTION
       autofs (the automounter, package autofs, service autofs) mounts file
       systems when someone first goes into their directory, and unmounts
       them again after a while of not being used. It is mostly used for NFS
       shares and home directories on a file server: nothing is mounted
       until it is needed, and a server that is down does not slow the boot.

       The master map lists mount points and, for each, the map file that
       says what to mount under it. RHEL 9 reads /etc/auto.master, which
       includes every file in /etc/auto.master.d whose name ends in
       .autofs. Put your own entries in such a file. After changes run
       systemctl restart autofs (and enable it so it starts at boot).

   Master map lines
           MOUNT-POINT  MAP-FILE  [OPTIONS]

       MOUNT-POINT
              An indirect map: the directory under which autofs creates
              entries (such as /shares or /rhome). autofs owns this directory;
              create nothing in it yourself.
              A direct map: the special name /- , and the map file then holds
              full paths.

       MAP-FILE
              The map file, usually /etc/auto.NAME.

       OPTIONS
              Mount options for every entry of the map, and autofs options
              such as --timeout=SECONDS (unmount after this idle time; 300 by
              default).

   Map file lines
           KEY  [-OPTIONS]  LOCATION

       KEY    In an indirect map, the name of the subdirectory under the
              mount point (/shares/KEY). * matches any name, and & in the
              location is replaced by that name (wildcard map). In a direct
              map, the full path of the mount point.

       OPTIONS
              Mount options, after a dash: -rw,sync or -fstype=nfs4,rw. The
              type is nfs when no fstype is given.

       LOCATION
              What to mount: HOST:/PATH for NFS, or :/dev/DEVICE for a local
              device.

EXAMPLES
       Mount the NFS export /exports/shared at /shares/work when it is used:

           dnf install -y autofs
           echo '/shares /etc/auto.shares' > /etc/auto.master.d/shares.autofs
           echo 'work -rw,sync nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/shared' > /etc/auto.shares
           systemctl enable --now autofs
           ls /shares/work

       Home directories for remoteuser1, remoteuser2 ... from the NFS server
       (wildcard map):

           echo '/rhome /etc/auto.rhome' > /etc/auto.master.d/rhome.autofs
           echo '* -rw nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/home/&' > /etc/auto.rhome
           systemctl restart autofs
           su - remoteuser1

       A direct map that mounts /mnt/public:

           echo '/- /etc/auto.direct' > /etc/auto.master.d/direct.autofs
           echo '/mnt/public -ro nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/public' > /etc/auto.direct
           systemctl restart autofs

NOTES
       ls /shares shows nothing until an entry is used: autofs mounts on
       access. Test by going into the full path (cd /shares/work or
       ls /shares/work), then check with mount | grep shares.

       autofs mount points must not also be in /etc/fstab.

FILES
       /etc/auto.master, /etc/auto.master.d/, /etc/autofs.conf

SEE ALSO
       autofs(8), automount(8), nfs(5), mount(8), showmount(8)
